      "id": "d3f794a9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The residual histogram is bell-shaped and centered at zero. Most Q–Q points follow the reference line; the upper tail bends slightly above the line, meaning a few games had more fouls than a normal distribution would predict. With more than 8,000 training games, this small tail departure has little effect on the coefficient estimates and intervals. Normality is approximately satisfied.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Identifiability"
      ],
      "id": "f5b77150"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from statsmodels.stats.outliers_influence import variance_inflation_factor\n",
        "\n",
        "## Rank check\n",
        "print('Matrix rank:', np.linalg.matrix_rank(X_train.to_numpy()), '| Number of columns:', X_train.shape[1])\n",
        "\n",
        "## VIF (computed with the constant included, constant row not shown)\n",
        "vifTable = pl.DataFrame({\n",
        "    'predictor': X_train.columns[1:].tolist(),\n",
        "    'VIF': [variance_inflation_factor(X_train.to_numpy().astype(float), i) for i in range(1, X_train.shape[1])],\n",
        "}).sort('VIF', descending = True)\n",
        "print('Mean VIF:', round(vifTable['VIF'].mean(), 2))\n",
        "with pl.Config(tbl_rows = 20):\n",
        "    display(vifTable.with_columns(pl.col('VIF').round(2)))"
      ],
      "id": "302353e1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The design matrix has full rank, so every coefficient can be estimated. The largest VIF is about 4.9 (`crew_fouls_per_game`), well below 10, and the mean VIF is about 2, not much more than 1. Multicollinearity is not a problem. The moderate correlation between crew and team foul averages (0.48) does not inflate the coefficient variances enough to affect interpretation. Identifiability is satisfied.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Summary:** all assumptions are satisfied or approximately satisfied, and no remedies (transformations or dropping predictors) are needed.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Evaluate\n",
        "\n",
        "## Models Compared\n",
        "\n",
        "We compared three models, all fit on the same training data and tested on the same held-out games:\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Model A — Teams only:** team foul averages, game context, and season. *No referee variables.* This answers \"how well can we do without scouting the referees?\"\n",
        "* **Model B — No season:** referee and team variables plus game context, but no season dummies.\n",
        "* **Model C — Full model:** all variables (selected)."
      ],
      "id": "f826c3c8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "crewCols = ['crew_fouls_per_game', 'crew_experience', 'crew_foul_diff', 'crew_home_win_pct']\n",
        "seasonCols = [c for c in X_train.columns if c.startswith('season_')]\n",
        "\n",
        "modelSpecs = {\n",
        "    'A: Teams only (no crew)': [c for c in X_train.columns if c not in crewCols],\n",
        "    'B: No season': [c for c in X_train.columns if c not in seasonCols],\n",
        "    'C: Full model': list(X_train.columns),\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "compareRows = []\n",
        "for name, cols in modelSpecs.items():\n",
        "    fit = sm.OLS(y_train, X_train[cols]).fit()\n",
        "    pred = fit.predict(X_test[cols])\n",
        "    compareRows.append({\n",
        "        'model': name,\n",
        "        'predictors': len(cols) - 1,\n",
        "        'R2': round(fit.rsquared, 3),\n",
        "        'adj_R2': round(fit.rsquared_adj, 3),\n",
        "        'test_RMSE': round(root_mean_squared_error(y_test, pred), 3),\n",
        "        'test_MSE': round(mean_squared_error(y_test, pred), 2),\n",
        "    })\n",
        "compareTable = pl.DataFrame(compareRows)\n",
        "compareTable"
      ],
      "id": "76998327",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The full model (C) has the highest adjusted $R^2$ and the lowest test RMSE and MSE, so we select it. Removing the referee variables (Model A) makes predictions worse on new games, which supports our main finding that the crew adds information beyond the teams. Removing season (Model B) also hurts fit, because league-wide foul levels change from year to year. The difference in test RMSE between models is modest in absolute terms (fractions of a foul), which reflects the large amount of game-to-game randomness in fouls that no pre-game model can explain.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Frequentist Results (OLS)"
      ],
      "id": "ee67e3ba"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ciTable = fit_05.conf_int()\n",
        "ols_results = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    'Estimate': fit_05.params,\n",
        "    'CI Lower': ciTable[0],\n",
        "    'CI Upper': ciTable[1],\n",
        "    'p-value': fit_05.pvalues,\n",
        "}).round(3)\n",
        "print(ols_results)\n",
        "print(f'\\nR-squared: {fit_05.rsquared:.3f} | Adj. R-squared: {fit_05.rsquared_adj:.3f}')\n",
        "print(f'F-statistic: {fit_05.fvalue:.1f} | Prob (F-statistic): {fit_05.f_pvalue:.3g}')"
      ],
      "id": "03827ddd",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Bayesian Results\n",
        "\n",
        "We also fit the same model as a Bayesian linear regression with flat (uniform) priors on all coefficients and an exponential prior on $\\sigma$. With flat priors and a large sample, we expect the Bayesian results to closely match OLS."
      ],
      "id": "22978b26"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "import pytensor\n",
        "pytensor.config.cxx = \"\"\n",
        "import bambi as bmb\n",
        "import arviz as az\n",
        "\n",
        "predictors = [c for c in X_train.columns if c != 'const']\n",
        "\n",
        "## Combine outcome and predictors for bambi\n",
        "nba_train = pd.concat([y_train, X_train.drop(columns = 'const')], axis = 1)\n",
        "\n",
        "ba_model = bmb.Model('total_fouls ~ ' + ' + '.join(predictors), data = nba_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "## Flat priors on coefficients, exponential prior on sigma\n",
        "priors_dict = {\n",
        "  'Intercept': bmb.Prior('Uniform', lower = -100, upper = 100),\n",
        "  **{term: bmb.Prior('Uniform', lower = -100, upper = 100) for term in predictors},\n",
        "  'sigma': bmb.Prior('Exponential', lam = 1)\n",
        "}\n",
        "ba_model.set_priors(priors = priors_dict)\n",
        "\n",
        "ba_fit = ba_model.fit(chains = 4, cores = 1, random_seed = 123)"
      ],
      "id": "3f87af39",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "baSum = az.summary(ba_fit, ci_kind = 'hdi', ci_prob = 0.95)\n",
        "baSum[['mean', 'sd', baSum.columns[2], baSum.columns[3], 'r_hat']].round(3)"
      ],
      "id": "f2aead54",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "All $\\hat{R}$ values are 1.00, which means the four chains agree and the sampler converged. Because MCMC uses random draws, Bayesian values may differ slightly in the last digit between renders. (Bambi applies the intercept prior to the intercept of centered predictors, so the reported intercept can fall outside the ±100 prior range.)\n",
        "\n",
        "## Overall Model Fit\n",
        "\n",
        "* **$R^2$ = 0.265 (adjusted 0.264).** The model explains about 26.5% of the variation in total fouls in the training games. The remaining variation is game-to-game randomness (game flow, blowouts, specific matchups, player-level foul trouble) that is not known before tip-off.\n",
        "* **F-statistic.** The p-value is essentially zero, so there is strong evidence that at least one predictor helps explain total fouls.\n",
        "* **Test RMSE ≈ 5.9 fouls.** On games the model did not see, predictions are off by about 6 fouls on average.\n",
        "\n",
        "An $R^2$ of 0.265 is modest, but our objective is not to predict every game exactly. It is to tell coaches whether to expect a higher- or lower-foul game than usual, and to measure how much the crew matters.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Interpreting the Parameter Estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "All slope interpretations hold all other variables fixed. Because the OLS confidence intervals and Bayesian credible intervals were nearly identical, we report both below and interpret them separately: a **95% confidence interval** comes from a procedure that captures the true parameter in 95% of repeated samples, while a **95% credible interval** means there is a 0.95 probability the parameter is in the interval, given the data, model, and priors.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Intercept (−111.56).** This is the predicted total fouls when every predictor is 0 (for example, a crew that calls 0 fouls per game), which is impossible. We do not interpret it.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Crew variables**\n",
        "\n",
        "* **`crew_fouls_per_game` (2.59).** For each additional foul per game in the crew's usual (season average) foul rate, the game has about 2.6 more total fouls. 95% CI: 2.43 to 2.76; 95% credible interval: 2.4 to 2.8. Both exclude zero (p < 0.001), so the effect is statistically significant. It is also practically significant: the effect is more than twice the size of either team's effect (about 1.2). A one-unit change in a three-referee crew average is a large change in crew terms; within the 2025–26 season, moving from a low-foul crew (10th percentile) to a high-foul crew (90th percentile) adds about 4 predicted fouls.\n",
        "* **`crew_experience` (0.004).** One more year of average crew experience changes total fouls by almost nothing. 95% CI: −0.025 to 0.034 (p = 0.770); credible interval: −0.024 to 0.034. Not statistically significant.\n",
        "* **`crew_foul_diff` (0.091).** Crews that call more fouls on road teams than home teams do not clearly call more or fewer fouls in total. 95% CI: −0.204 to 0.387 (p = 0.546); credible interval: −0.22 to 0.38. Not statistically significant.\n",
        "* **`crew_home_win_pct` (0.224).** This is a proportion, so one unit is a change from 0% to 100% home wins. Even this extreme change has no clear effect. 95% CI: −2.83 to 3.28 (p = 0.885); credible interval: −2.8 to 3.3. Not statistically significant.\n",
        "\n",
        "These three variables are kept as controls. Learning that experience and home bias do not change the total number of fouls is itself useful: what matters about a crew is its foul rate, not its experience or home lean.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Team variables**\n",
        "\n",
        "* **`home_team_fouls` (1.21).** If the home team averages 1 more foul per game, the game has about 1.2 more total fouls. 95% CI: 1.11 to 1.31; credible interval: 1.1 to 1.3. Statistically significant.\n",
        "* **`away_team_fouls` (1.19).** If the away team averages 1 more foul per game, the game has about 1.2 more total fouls. 95% CI: 1.08 to 1.29; credible interval: 1.1 to 1.3. Statistically significant.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Game context**\n",
        "\n",
        "* **`overtime` (4.85).** Each overtime period adds about 4.8 total fouls, consistent with five extra minutes of play. 95% CI: 4.38 to 5.32; credible interval: 4.4 to 5.3. Statistically significant.\n",
        "* **`late_season` (−1.56).** Games in the last two weeks of the season have about 1.6 fewer total fouls than other games. 95% CI: −1.99 to −1.14; credible interval: about −2.0 to −1.1. Statistically significant. This may reflect resting players and less competitive games.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Season dummies (reference level: 2017–18)**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Season | Estimate | 95% CI | Significant? |\n",
        "|:--|--:|:--|:--|\n",
        "| 2018–19 | −5.52 | −6.16 to −4.89 | Yes (p < 0.001) |\n",
        "| 2019–20 | −4.75 | −5.38 to −4.11 | Yes (p < 0.001) |\n",
        "| 2020–21 | 3.64 | 3.04 to 4.24 | Yes (p < 0.001) |\n",
        "| 2021–22 | 1.71 | 1.15 to 2.27 | Yes (p < 0.001) |\n",
        "| 2022–23 | −0.24 | −0.77 to 0.28 | No (p = 0.364) |\n",
        "| 2023–24 | 6.73 | 6.05 to 7.42 | Yes (p < 0.001) |\n",
        "| 2024–25 | 7.57 | 6.85 to 8.29 | Yes (p < 0.001) |\n",
        "| 2025–26 | 0.49 | −0.05 to 1.02 | No (p = 0.073) |\n",
        "\n",
        "The season estimates are *adjusted* differences from 2017–18 after accounting for the crew and team averages. Because crew and team averages already move with league-wide foul levels each season, these dummies do not match the raw season boxplots (for example, 2023–24 has fewer raw fouls but a positive adjusted estimate). They should be read as season adjustments, not as raw season averages. Bayesian credible intervals agreed with OLS in direction and significance for every season.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Visualizing the Interval Estimates"
      ],
      "id": "1349cb6f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "niceNames = {\n",
        "  'crew_fouls_per_game': 'Crew: usual fouls called',\n",
        "  'home_team_fouls': 'Home team: usual fouls',\n",
        "  'away_team_fouls': 'Away team: usual fouls',\n",
        "  'overtime': 'Each overtime period',\n",
        "  'late_season': 'Last 2 weeks of season',\n",
        "  'crew_experience': 'Crew: years of experience',\n",
        "  'crew_foul_diff': 'Crew: road-team foul bias',\n",
        "  'crew_home_win_pct': 'Crew: home-team win %',\n",
        "}\n",
        "plotOrder = list(niceNames.values())[::-1]\n",
        "\n",
        "olsDf = pl.DataFrame({\n",
        "  'term': fit_05.params.index.tolist(),\n",
        "  'coef': fit_05.params.tolist(),\n",
        "  'low': ciTable[0].tolist(),\n",
        "  'high': ciTable[1].tolist(),\n",
        "})\n",
        "bayesDf = pl.DataFrame({\n",
        "  'term': baSum.index.tolist(),\n",
        "  'coef': baSum.iloc[:, 0].tolist(),\n",
        "  'low': baSum.iloc[:, 2].tolist(),\n",
        "  'high': baSum.iloc[:, 3].tolist(),\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "bothDf = (pl.concat([\n",
        "    olsDf.with_columns(method = pl.lit('OLS (confidence interval)')),\n",
        "    bayesDf.with_columns(method = pl.lit('Bayesian (credible interval)')),\n",
        "  ])\n",
        "  ## crew_home_win_pct has a very wide interval (0-1 scale), so it is left off the chart\n",
        "  .filter(pl.col('term').is_in([k for k in niceNames if k != 'crew_home_win_pct']))\n",
        "  .with_columns(label = pl.col('term').replace(niceNames))\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "(ggplot(bothDf, aes(x = 'coef', y = 'label'))\n",
        "  + geom_point(size = 2.5, color = nbaGreen)\n",
        "  + geom_errorbarh(aes(xmin = 'low', xmax = 'high'), height = 0.2, color = nbaGreen)\n",
        "  + geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red', linetype = '--')\n",
        "  + facet_wrap('~method')\n",
        "  + scale_y_discrete(limits = [l for l in plotOrder if l != 'Crew: home-team win %'])\n",
        "  + labs(x = 'Change in total fouls (95% interval)', y = '',\n",
        "         title = 'Crews matter more than teams: two methods, same answer')\n",
        "  + nbaTheme\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "bbf558aa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
