    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The model predicted 39 fouls with a 95% prediction interval of about 28 to 51. The actual game had 50 fouls, inside the interval but well above the point prediction. This is one game, and it was a **preseason** game, which is outside the regular-season data the model was trained on (preseason rotations and officiating can differ). It illustrates both how the model would be used and why the prediction interval, not just the point estimate, should guide planning.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Recommendations\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Scout the referees every game day.** Check the crew assignment at 9 AM ET and compute the crew's foul rate from the previous season (or season to date).\n",
        "2. **High-foul crew expected:** plan for about 4 more fouls than with a low-foul crew. Manage minutes for foul-prone players, keep fouls-to-give in mind late in quarters, and play safer, less physical defense.\n",
        "3. **Low-foul crew expected:** a more physical defensive approach carries less foul risk.\n",
        "4. **Ignore crew experience and home/road bias** for planning total fouls; neither had a measurable effect.\n",
        "5. **Use ranges, not single numbers.** Any single game can land roughly ±11 fouls from the prediction, so the model should shift planning, not replace in-game judgment.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Limitations and Next Steps\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Pre-game inputs only.** Refit the model using prior-season or season-to-date referee and team averages so the in-sample estimates match the real pre-game use and remove the leakage from full-season averages.\n",
        "* **More detailed data:** player fouls and minutes, foul types (shooting, offensive, loose-ball), team strategy data, and preseason/playoff games.\n",
        "* **Model upgrades:** a Poisson regression for count outcomes, or a model with individual referee effects instead of a crew average.\n",
        "* **Association, not causation.** The model shows that crews with higher foul rates are associated with more fouls; it does not prove that assigning a different crew would change a specific game by that amount."
      ],
      "id": "80a7e5bb"
    }
